FILE: app/src/limited-orbit.ts
```ts
import type { ArcRotateCamera, SceneContext } from "@babylonjs/lite";
import { attachControl, setCameraLimits } from "@babylonjs/lite";

export function attachLimitedOrbit(
  camera: ArcRotateCamera,
  canvas: HTMLCanvasElement,
  scene: SceneContext,
  minDistance: number,
  maxDistance: number
): () => void {
  const disposeControl = attachControl(camera, canvas, scene);
  const disposeLimits = setCameraLimits(
    camera,
    { lowerRadiusLimit: minDistance, upperRadiusLimit: maxDistance },
    scene
  );

  return () => {
    disposeControl();
    disposeLimits();
  };
}
```

I composed `attachLimitedOrbit` from the library's own `attachControl` (which gives the standard drag-to-orbit, wheel, and pinch controls) and `setCameraLimits` (which clamps the radius to the given range on every write and immediately brings the pose inside on call). The returned function disposes both, removing all canvas listeners and per-frame hooks. I'm confident in this approach; the only thing I wasn't 100% sure of is whether `@babylonjs/lite` re-exports `attachControl` and `setCameraLimits` at its top level versus a sub-path, but given the package name in your import statement and the fact that these are the primary camera-control APIs, a top-level import is the natural expectation.
